> Coasts, Sea-Level Rise, and Real Estate Markets
> The Economics Chapter of the Fifth National Climate Assessment (Hsiang et al., 2023) addresses coastal climate risk primarily through aggregate damage functions and broad vulnerability framing. It does not provide a focused treatment of real estate market dynamics, financial sector exposures, or the economics of coastal adaptation. NCA5 Ch. 9 also identifies key economic knowledge gaps: the financial consequences of chronic inundation, the systemic risks embedded in insurance and mortgage markets, and the distributional effects of adaptation policy are flagged as priority areas requiring additional research.
> This section highlights four interconnected topics that together constitute the frontier of coastal climate economics: direct impacts on property values, indirect impacts through insurance and mortgage markets, adaptation economics, and climate-induced migration.
> Direct Impacts on Property Values/ Asset Prices
> There is a long tradition of using hedonic pricing to estimate how coastal flood risk is capitalized into property values. NCA5 acknowledges this literature but notes that capitalization is partial, heterogeneous, and lagged relative to the underlying physical risk trajectory. Recent work has substantially advanced causal identification.
> •	Keys and Mulder (2020) document 5–10% price declines in high-exposure Florida tracts after 2016, with little evidence that lender standards drove the adjustment.
> •	Tyndall (2023) provides repeat-sales evidence from Long Island. Tarui et al. (2023) and Khan et al. (2025) extend this to Hawaiʻi, finding an annual appreciation penalty of 0.8% for exposed properties moderated by buyer origin. (may be too specific for this review?)
> •	Fairweather et al. (2024) conduct a large-scale natural field experiment through Redfin in which 17.5 million users were randomly assigned to receive home-specific flood risk information, allowing for causal identification of how climate risk disclosure shapes housing market behavior. They find that flood risk information affects every stage of the home-buying process — search, bidding, and final purchase — with buyers in high-risk areas showing the strongest behavioral response and demonstrating willingness to trade off property amenities for lower flood exposure. This paper provides early experimental evidence that climate adaptation in housing markets can be proactive and forward-looking rather than purely reactive to experienced losses.
> Key open questions include whether commercial real estate and rental markets exhibit similar patterns, whether disclosure laws accelerate or smooth price adjustment, and how buyer heterogeneity in risk beliefs shapes market dynamics.
> Fairweather, D., Kahn, M.E., Metcalfe, R.D., and Sandoval-Olascoaga, S. (2024). Expecting climate change: A nationwide field experiment in the housing market. NBER Working Paper 33119.
> Keys, B.J. and Mulder, P. (2020). Neglected no more: Housing markets, mortgage lending, and sea level rise. NBER Working Paper 27930. https://www.nber.org/papers/w27930
> Khan, M.T., Tarui, N., Newfield, C., and Coffman, M. (2025). Sea level rise impacts on residential real estate value in Hawaiʻi. Climatic Change, 178, article 210. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10584-025-04020-4
> Tarui, N., Urbanski, S., Lam, Q.L., Coffman, M., and Newfield, C. (2023). Sea level rise risk interactions with coastal property values: A case study of Oʻahu, Hawaiʻi. Climatic Change, 176(9).
> Tyndall, J.C. (2023). Sea level rise and home prices: Evidence from Long Island. Journal of Real Estate Finance and Economics, 67(4), 579–605. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11146-021-09868-8
> Insurance, Mortgage Markets, and Fiscal Stress (Indirect Impacts)
> NCA5 Ch. 9 identifies financial system exposure to SLR as an emerging concern within its discussion of property and infrastructure risk, but treats insurance and mortgage market dynamics only in passing — there is no systematic economic analysis of how risk mispricing propagates through these institutions. NCA Ch. 19 explicitly flags financial sector exposure and the potential for abrupt market corrections as priority research gaps, noting that most existing work focuses on physical damages rather than secondary financial transmission channels. The economics literature has now moved substantially ahead of NCA5 on this front, documenting specific mechanisms of risk transfer and underpricing across three interconnected channels: flood insurance, mortgage markets, and local fiscal stress.
> •	The NFIP has long underpriced coastal flood risk. Gourevitch et al. (2023) provide a comprehensive analysis of the resulting mispricing: comparing observed property prices against what prices would be under efficient risk pricing, they estimate that flood-exposed residential properties are overvalued by $121–$237 billion nationally, depending on the assumed discount rate.
> •	Private insurers are now withdrawing from the highest-exposure markets, particularly in Florida, California, and Gulf states, leaving households either uninsured or reliant on last-resort state programs. Keys and Mulder (2024) exploit mortgage escrow data — isolating the insurance component from over 47 million mortgage payments — to document a 33% nominal (13% real) increase in average homeowner premiums between 2020 and 2023, with the steepest increases in climate-exposed zip codes. A key mechanism is the pass-through of reinsurance costs: as catastrophic risk was repriced in global capital markets (driven by wildfire and hurricane losses), those costs were rapidly transmitted to household premiums, suggesting that coastal housing affordability is now directly linked to conditions in international reinsurance markets.
> •	Wagner (2022) tests for adverse selection in NFIP flood insurance markets using quasi-experimental variation in premiums created by the 2012 Biggert-Waters and 2014 HFIAA reforms. In high-risk flood zones, fewer than 60% of homeowners purchase insurance even though subsidized premiums are only two-thirds of their own expected payouts — indicating large non-financial frictions (risk under-perception, present bias) that compound the underinsurance problem.
> •	Keenan and Bradt (2020) introduce the concept of "underwaterwriting" — institutional and informational barriers that prevent accurate climate risk assessment in mortgage origination — and provide empirical evidence that concentrated local lenders in the Southeast Atlantic and Gulf Coast regions are securitizing and selling off flood-exposed mortgages at higher rates, transferring risk to GSEs and ultimately to taxpayers.
> •	Giglio et al. (2021) leverage the unique structure of real estate markets — where freeholds and leaseholds exist for the same properties — to non-parametrically estimate the term structure of discount rates at horizons of 100+ years. They find that the discount rate for payoffs over very long horizons (relevant to end-of-century sea level projections) reaches only ~2.6%, implying that long-run climate risks should be capitalized into current property prices. The empirical finding that coastal properties do not appear to be discounting SLR at actuarially fair rates (as documented in the hedonic literature) is not explained by high long-run discount rates.
> Giglio, S., Maggiori, M., Rao, K., Stroebel, J., and Weber, A. (2021). Climate change and long-run discount rates: Evidence from real estate. Review of Financial Studies, 34(8), 3527–3571. https://doi.org/10.1093/rfs/hhab032
> Gourevitch, J.D., Kousky, C., Liao, Y., Nolte, C., Pollack, A.B., Porter, J.R., and Weill, J.A. (2023). Unpriced climate risk and the potential consequences of overvaluation in US housing markets. Nature Climate Change, 13, 250–257. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41558-023-01594-8
> Keenan, J.M. and Bradt, J.T. (2020). Underwaterwriting: From theory to empiricism in regional mortgage markets in the US. Climatic Change, 162, 2043–2067. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10584-020-02734-1
> Keys, B.J. and Mulder, P. (2024). Property insurance and disaster risk: New evidence from mortgage escrow data. NBER Working Paper 32579. https://doi.org/10.3386/w32579
> Wagner, K.R.H. (2022). Adaptation and adverse selection in markets for natural disaster insurance. American Economic Journal: Economic Policy, 14(3), 380–421. https://doi.org/10.1257/pol.20200378
>  
> Adaptation: Protection, Managed Retreat, and Buyouts
> NCA5 Ch. 9 describes the three adaptation categories for coastal areas — protection, accommodation, and retreat — but does not provide systematic economic evaluation of their costs, benefits, or distributional consequences and flags this as a major gap. The economic literature has made substantial progress on the protection and retreat ends of the spectrum, while accommodation (e.g., elevation of structures, floodproofing, land use zoning) remains the least-studied category.
> •	Beach nourishment is the dominant form of soft protection on the US Atlantic and Gulf coasts, at a combined federal and local cost of hundreds of millions of dollars annually. McNamara et al. (2024) develop a coupled Coastal Housing and Ocean Model (C-HOM) to show that nourishment subsidies delay the price signal from SLR that should otherwise drive efficient reallocation away from high-risk coastal zones. Properties in locations that adapt with nourishment have elevated prices relative to their long-run physical risk. The model further shows that subsidized protection stimulates additional development in vulnerable areas — consistent with the "development paradox" in climate adaptation, in which risk-reduction interventions increase exposure by attracting investment to protected areas.
> •	Government buyout and acquisition programs remain underutilized as a policy for managed retreat and their economic consequences are poorly understood. Hashida and Dundas (2023) study a voluntary buyout program in New York's coastal counties and find spatially heterogeneous housing market spillovers. Properties within 100 meters of a buyout site experience price declines of 10–20%, while properties near acquisitions where the government redevelops the site face larger, longer-range negative effects persisting up to 1,200 meters.  Guo, Liao, and Miao (2023) study the same NY Rising program but account for Hurricane Sandy's confounding damage and find that acquisitions in particular generate positive property value spillovers, improve nearby business performance, and attract higher-income buyers.
> •	Hashida and Dundas (2024) examine participation in New Jersey's Blue Acres buyout program and find that property tax revenue pressures create principal-agent problems at the municipal level. Municipalities that depend on property taxes are less likely to implement successful buyouts, even when households want to participate, because each buyout reduces the tax base. This fiscal barrier disproportionately affects communities with lower property values and fewer alternative revenue sources adding a distributional dimension to the managed retreat challenge.
> •	Keeler, Mullin, McNamara, and Smith (2022) propose buyouts with rentbacks (BWRs) as a mechanism to overcome the barrier of forced immediate displacement of residents who sell to the government. Under BWR, households sell their property but retain the right to remain as renters until a future announced departure date, separating the transfer of ownership (and liability) from the timing of physical relocation. A key unresolved question is whether BWR-generated rental revenue is sufficient to finance buyouts at scale, and how departure date triggers should be set under uncertainty about future SLR trajectories.
> Guo, W., Liao, Y., and Miao, Q. (2023). Managed retreat and flood recovery: The local economic impacts of a buyout and acquisition program. RFF Working Paper 23-44. https://www.rff.org/publications/working-papers/flood-recovery-local-economic-impacts-of-buyout-and-acquisition-hurricane-sandy-new-york/
> Hashida, Y. and Dundas, S.J. (2023). The effects of a voluntary property buyout and acquisition program on coastal housing markets: Evidence from New York. Journal of Environmental Economics and Management, 121, 102873. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jeem.2023.102873
> Hashida, Y. and Dundas, S.J. (2024). Barriers to coastal managed retreat: Evidence from New Jersey's Blue Acres program. Marine Resource Economics, 39(3), 179–205. https://doi.org/10.1086/729868
> Keeler, A.G., Mullin, M., McNamara, D.E., and Smith, M.D. (2022). Buyouts with rentbacks: A policy proposal for managing coastal retreat. Journal of Environmental Studies and Sciences. https://doi.org/10.1007/s13412-022-00762-0
> McNamara, D.E., Smith, M.D., Williams, Z., Gopalakrishnan, S., and Landry, C.E. (2024). Policy and market forces delay real estate price declines on the US coast. Nature Communications, 15, 2209. https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-024-01273-4
>  
> Migration: Displacement, Redistribution, and Receiving Communities
> NCA5 identifies coastal displacement as a consequence of SLR but does not provide systematic economic analysis of migration dynamics, receiving community impacts, or the distributional consequences of displacement —one of the clearest gaps in the treatment of coastal economics.
> •	Hauer (2017) established that up to 13 million Americans face displacement under unmitigated SLR by 2100. Hauer, Jacobs & Kulp (2024) extend this framework using matrix population models to show that demographic amplification compounds direct displacement 5- to 18-fold. Because younger, working-age households are most likely to migrate, SLR-origin communities face accelerated population aging while destination communities must absorb far larger population pressures than direct migrant measures suggest.
> •	Hauer et al. (2020) maps the mechanisms linking SLR hazards to human mobility — distinguishing direct inundation from slower-onset hazards (salinity intrusion, erosion, nuisance flooding) — and shows that mobility responses are mediated by socioeconomic and demographic factors rather than being automatic responses to physical exposure, providing the conceptual foundation for the empirical literature that follows.
> •	Duijndam et al. (2022) conduct a systematic review finding that flooding is not automatically associated with increased migration and that socioeconomic resources are the primary mediator of whether and where households relocate — raising "trapped populations" concerns for low-income coastal residents who lack the capital to move. Duijndam et al. (2025) advance this work with original survey data from Argentina, France, Mozambique, and the United States, confirming cross-nationally that migration propensity under flood risk is higher in wealthier settings, with place attachment, self-efficacy, and age as consistent determinants across contexts.
> The economics of receiving communities — housing market pressures from climate in-migration, public service demands, and municipal fiscal effects — remains largely unstudied and represents a key frontier for future research.
> Duijndam, S.J., Botzen, W.J.W., Hagedoorn, L.C., and Aerts, J.C.J.H. (2022). Anticipating sea-level rise and human migration: A review of empirical evidence and avenues for future research. WIREs Climate Change, 13(1), e747. https://doi.org/10.1002/wcc.747
> Duijndam, S.J., Botzen, W.J.W., Hagedoorn, L.C., Ton, M., de Bruijn, J., Carretero, S., Dachary-Bernard, J., Rulleau, B., and Aerts, J.C.J.H. (2025). Global determinants of coastal migration under climate change. Nature Communications, 16, 6866. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-59199-y
> Hauer, M.E. (2017). Migration induced by sea-level rise could reshape the US population landscape. Nature Climate Change, 7, 321–325. https://doi.org/10.1038/nclimate3271
> Hauer, M.E., Fussell, E., Mueller, V., Burkett, M., Call, M., Abel, K., McLeman, R., and Wrathall, D. (2020). Sea-level rise and human migration. Nature Reviews Earth & Environment, 1, 28–39. https://doi.org/10.1038/s43017-019-0002-9
> Hauer, M.E., Jacobs, S.A., and Kulp, S.A. (2024). Climate migration amplifies demographic change and population aging. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 121(3), e2206192119. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2206192119
